# Conventional Commits

```text
type(optional-scope): subject

optional body

optional footer
```

- Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`, `build`, `ci`. A speed or memory change is `feat` or `fix` with its measurement in the body; there is no `perf` type.
- Scope: English, a short noun for the area. Examples, not a closed list: `core`, `reactive`, `layout`, `text`, `render`, `d3d11`, `win32`, `testing`, `primitives`, `fluent`, `gallery`, `rules`, `deps`.
- Subject: English, imperative, no trailing period. The type and scope are always English (`.cursor/rules/language.mdc`).
- Breaking change: `type(scope)!: subject` and/or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer.
- A closing keyword (`Closes #N`) never goes in a commit body; the squash message concatenates commit bodies (`.cursor/rules/github-closing.mdc`). Write `Refs #N` or `Part of #N`. From #71 to #85 the agent wrote `Closes #N` in commit bodies and in a squash message without reading this rule; the Issues closed were the PRs' own, so nothing closed early.
- Every commit takes one of the types above, the ones in the middle of a row too. There is no `wip`: in L1a four `wip(layout)` commits left the owner to rewrite the squash message (a review found it). A local commit that is not pushed yet takes a type too: in P02e a `wip` commit and a `perf-free` one had to be folded into one before the push (PR #611).

```text
feat(reactive): flush effects in depth order at the outermost batch
fix(d3d11): release every back-buffer view before ResizeBuffers
chore(rules): record the ref-lifetime rule from the S2 retrospective
```
